Pattern Processing
The Raspberry Pi checks that the selected image:

is a valid image readable as RGB;
has width equal to 48 pixels;
contains exactly three distinct colours.

The image pixels are read sequentially. The first distinct colour is mapped to C1, the second to C2, and the third to C3.

Three custom mechanical arms were designed to connect the servomotors to the yarn-selection mechanism. Each arm converts the angular position of one servo into the displacement required to activate or release one colour channel.
The use of custom components makes it possible to:

adapt the actuator geometry to the machine structure;
obtain repeatable motion for the three yarn channels;
reproduce the mechanism through 3D printing;
calibrate mechanical movement through firmware angles.

7. Prepare the Hardware
Before starting a real test, verify that:

the Raspberry Pi is powered;
the GFX HAT display is connected to the Raspberry Pi;
the ESP32 is connected to the Raspberry Pi through its USB cable;
the three servomotors and the stepper are supplied by the external power supply;
all the GND are connected;
the microswitch is connected to the ESP32.

See Wiring and Power Supply for the complete connections.

12. Start Production
Press PLUS (AVVIA).
The Raspberry Pi:

validates the PNG file;
checks that the image is 48 pixels wide;
checks that the image contains exactly three RGB colours;
opens the USB serial communication with the ESP32;
sends the command corresponding to the first pixel.
